From: François Guerraz <kubrick@fgv6.net>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: ppe: S-R register encoders and
 sequences

The S-R mode gives each (switch port, form, VLAN) class an identity of
its own: an XLT rule on the conduit's PPE port that matches the
switch's S-tag of the port (3900 + p) and the class's C-tag, and an
ingress L3 interface that the rule's action hands the frame. A flow
keyed on that interface is hit only by its class's frames. For
downloads the nexthop inserts the S-tag of the port (3910 + p) and the
C-tag of the VLAN. This patch adds the register side of all that,
with no caller yet.

ppe_sr_hw.c encodes, in pure code (no kernel call, tested in user
space): the XLT rule (S-tagged with an upload S-VID, plus either no
C-tag and EtherType 0x0800, or a C-tag with the class's VID; the
EtherType key keeps a client's nested 802.1ad tag from taking U(p)'s
identity), the action (L3 interface and counter, no tag, PCP or VSI
change), the inert test of an L3 interface and its copy (the
conduit's, without IPv6 routing, PPPoE, the DMAC check bypass and the
VPN), and the fields S-R moves: the egress S-TPID (0x8899 to 0x88a8),
the CPU port's egress tag modes (0x134 to 0x11f), the conduit's L3
binding (valid bit cleared, index kept) and its parser role (core).
The encodings are held against the words the PPE gate measured on a
GL-BE9300.

ppe_sr_seq.c runs the sequences on struct ppe_sr_io (register access,
the flows, the rtl8_4s state word's ppe byte, logging), in pure code
too. Every write is read back; a field or entry is moved only from the
value it is expected to hold, and each step records that it may have
written before it writes. The arm: read-only prechecks and the
identities' entries (lowest free XLT entry in 0-63 whose counter no
valid rule's action names, lowest inert L3 interface in 32-255), the
switch claimed, every flow dropped, then S-TPID, CPU port, identities
(L3 interface copy, counter zero, action, rule last), binding and the
parser role last, then a full verify. A failure rolls back what was
recorded, role first, and releases the claim; a failed rollback, like
a stuck flow or a failed revert later, latches FAULT: the claim and
every register stay until reboot. The disarm drops every flow and
puts back the role first (nothing more if that fails, so a core role
never meets an E CPU port), then the rest, each step attempted. A
resync plans the identity changes against a new set of classes
(removals, recopies after a conduit change, additions; live
identities are never displaced over budget), executes them from the
arm's record of what is free without reading foreign entries, and
takes back a failed addition alone. Also a boot check of S-R's
registers, the identities' counters and the names for status.

The nexthop encoder moves out of the register write into
ppe_nexthop_entry_encode()/_decode(). The pool reads every new entry
back (one that reads otherwise is zeroed and declined), and never hands
out index 0: enable programs it as a CPU sink (CPU port, IP_TO_ME), so
that a host entry's DST_INFO 0, should hardware take it for a nexthop
on a frame no flow matched, reaches the CPU.

The register definitions added: XLT rule and action (with the C-VID
and EtherType keys), XLT and L3 interface counters, ingress bridge
type, port default VID and VLAN config, the tunnel parser's port role,
and the L3 binding's VSI.

